## Claim 4 — linear, quadratic, and singular controls

**CONFIRMED.**

The identity `L_K=K\{K,2psi-3K phi\}` is exact, not a congruence, so a linearized bracket is never a nonzero constant. `Res_z(K(1,z),c)=c^3`. Domain gauges cannot enlarge the image. This is tangent-only.

Under zero first-order bracket one has `psi=(3/2)K phi`, and Euler reduces `q_2` to `9 phi^2 K_z\pmod{K}` at `t=1`. On `Delta\neq 0`, with constant row and both boundaries still absent, this is `K\mid phi`, which is exactly the three rows `(4.6)` and leaves the two common-cubic tangents. Split-control residues are `(0,18,18)`. If the constant row or a boundary first appears at this order, the implication fails; DS is the delayed control (`lambda^3` then `lambda^7`).

The two jets reconstruct, vanish over `k[e]/(e^3)`, satisfy the selected-root boundaries, and are not characteristic-zero Keller pairs.

