## Claim 7 — conditional pure-DS closure

**CONFIRMED.** No valuation case was omitted; no counterexample is frozen.

Pure entry is hypothesized, not derived. Weighted Euler reconstructs `J_{(\lambda,z)}=567\lambda^6` and `(lambda^7)'=j/(81s)`. Descent `lambda=s^2 q` converts this to `(7.4)`.

Finite places, complete lattice: `gcd(5,7)=1` so every integer solution of `5e+7n=1` is `e=3+7k`, `n=-2-5k`. The constraints `e>0` and `2e+3n\neq 0` force `k\geq 1`. Cancellation `2e+3n=0` has valuation `\geq e/3>0` (identically zero on pure monomials; valuation exactly `1` on the first unit perturbation of `(e,n)=(3,-2)`). Places with `e=0` force `n=0`. If `h` has no zero then `h` and `q` are constant and the left-hand side of `(7.4)` vanishes. Logarithmic derivatives at infinity give a polynomial of degree `r-1` with leading coefficient containing `-\sum k_i\neq 0`, so `r=1`. Historical `3\mid\deg(h)` forces `3\mid k` and the cube-core form `(7.8)`. There is no noncube/Kummer solution *on this residue*. Smallest control: `h=x^{24}`, `s=x^8`, `q=x^{-17}`, `lambda=x^{-1}`, source factor `-567`.

Boundaries: the four valuations in `f_lambda(r)`, shifted by `3ell`, are `(3w,2w,w,0)` with `w=2nu+ell`. If `w\neq 0` the minimum is unique and negative, a pole. If `w=0` all terms of `f` and of `g` balance, so regularity forces a common root of `f_1,g_1`, contradicting the resultant. Odd `ell` never balances. Poles of `r` away from the pole of `lambda`, and `r\equiv 0`, are likewise poles of `f_lambda(r)`.

Cube-mismatch and extra lower-weight constants remain open, as claimed.

